Sec. 40001. This section appropriates $24,593,500,000 to the Coast Guard for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to procure new operational assets and maintain existing systems. Specific allocations include $4,300,000,000 for Offshore Patrol Cutters, $4,300,000,000 for Polar Security Cutters, and $3,500,000,000 for Arctic Security Cutters. Additionally, $4,379,000,000 is designated for shore facility improvements, including $500,000,000 for the Coast Guard Yard and $425,000,000 for enlisted boot camp facilities.

Sec. 40002. This section extends the Federal Communications Commission’s general auction authority for spectrum licenses until September 30, 2034, excluding the 3.1–3.45 GHz and 7.4–8.4 GHz bands. It mandates the auction of at least 300 MHz of spectrum, including 100 MHz in the 3.98–4.2 GHz band, within two years of enactment. The Assistant Secretary of Commerce for Communications and Information must identify an additional 500 MHz of federal frequencies for reallocation to commercial use, with at least 200 MHz identified within two years. The President retains the authority to modify or withdraw proposed frequencies for national security reasons up to 60 days before an auction.

Sec. 40003. This section appropriates funds to the Federal Aviation Administration for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to improve air traffic control infrastructure and safety. Key allocations include $4,750,000,000 for telecommunications modernization, $3,000,000,000 for radar replacement, and $1,900,000,000 to construct a new air route traffic control center (ARTCC). The FAA must also close or consolidate at least 10 existing ARTCCs and recapitalize terminal radar approach control facilities (TRACONs) using $1,000,000,000.

Sec. 40004. This section establishes a user fee for space launch and reentry licenses issued by the Secretary of Transportation, effective for launches in 2026 and subsequent years. The fee is the lesser of a per-pound payload charge or a flat cap, starting at $0.25 per pound or $30,000 in 2026 and increasing annually through 2033 before adjusting for inflation. Proceeds are deposited into the Office of Commercial Space Transportation Launch and Reentry Licensing and Permitting Fund, with 70% available for FAA expenses without further appropriation.

Sec. 40005. This section appropriates $9,995,000,000 to NASA for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2032, to fund Mars, Artemis, and Moon-to-Mars programs. Allocations include $700,000,000 for a commercial Mars telecommunications orbiter, $2,600,000,000 for the Gateway lunar station, and $4,100,000,000 for Space Launch System operations for Artemis IV and V. Additionally, $1,000,000,000 is designated for infrastructure improvements at NASA’s manned spaceflight centers, including specific amounts for Kennedy, Johnson, and Marshall Space Centers.

Sec. 40006. This section amends the Corporate Average Fuel Economy (CAFE) civil penalty provisions by reducing the penalty rates to $0.00 per vehicle for both individual and fleet non-compliance. The amendment takes effect immediately upon enactment and applies to all model years for which the Secretary of Transportation has not yet issued a penalty notification. This effectively eliminates civil monetary penalties for manufacturers failing to meet CAFE standards for those specified model years.

Sec. 40007. This section amends the lease agreement for Metropolitan Washington Airports, requiring the Airports Authority to pay the Treasury $3,000,000 (in 1987 dollars) annually from 1987 through 2026. Starting in 2027, the annual payment increases to $15,000,000 (in 2027 dollars), with a requirement to renegotiate the lease level at least every 10 years. The renegotiation must ensure that the payment never falls below the $15,000,000 (2027 dollars) threshold in any subsequent year.

Sec. 40008. This section rescinds any unobligated balances of funds previously appropriated or made available to the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) under sections 40001 through 40004 of Public Law 117-169. The rescission applies specifically to amounts that have not yet been obligated by the agency. This action reduces the available funding for NOAA programs covered by the referenced prior legislation.

Sec. 40009. This section amends the Travel Promotion Act of 2009 to reduce the annual transfer to the Travel Promotion Fund from $100,000,000 to $20,000,000. The change modifies subsection (d)(2)(B) of the Act, significantly decreasing the federal financial support allocated for travel promotion activities. This reduction impacts the funding available for international travel marketing and related initiatives.

Sec. 40010. This section rescinds any unobligated balances of funds made available for alternative fuel and low-emission aviation technology under section 40007(a) of title IV of Public Law 117-169. The rescission applies to amounts that have not yet been obligated for these specific aviation technology initiatives. This action reduces the remaining funding available for the development and deployment of alternative aviation fuels and technologies.

Sec. 40011. This section permanently rescinds $850,000,000 from the unobligated balances of the Public Wireless Supply Chain Innovation Fund established under the CHIPS Act of 2022. The funds were originally made available under section 106(a) of Public Law 117-167. This rescission reduces the total amount available for wireless supply chain innovation projects by the specified quantity. [end of text]


